The previous bound lower bound $\frac{f}{n-2f}$ has strictly lower order than our $\frac{\hat{f}}{n-f-\hat{f}}$ under order-level overestimation $\hat{f}\gg f$ (i.e., $\hat{f}$ has higher order than that of $f$). This covers many cases  (**NOT only edge cases**), for example,

(1) When $f=\mathcal{O}(1)$, while $\hat{f}=\mathcal{O}(n^a)$ ($0<a\le 1$) or $\hat{f}=\mathcal{O}(\log^a n)$ ($a>0$); 

(2) When $f=\mathcal{O}(\log^b n)$ ($b>0$), while $\hat{f}=\log^a n$ ($a>b$) or $\hat{f}=\mathcal{O}(n^a)$ ($0<a\le 1$); 

(3) When $f=\mathcal{O}(n^a)$ ($0<a<1$) while $\hat{f}=\mathcal{O}(n^b)$ ($a<b\le 1$).
